      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 13,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 36,
          "character": 4
        }
      },
      "title": "The README is out of date",
      "description": "**What it is:** the architecture picture in your README.\n\n**How it works (today):** the code has moved on since this was drawn:\n- **Feb 9, 2026** — the Hono API moved *inside* the Next.js app. The live API no longer runs as its own server on port 3001.\n- **Jan 12, 2026** — the agent switched from CrewAI to LangGraph. This box still says CrewAI.\n- **Jan 7, 2026** — login moved to Neon Auth. The Tech Stack table below still says Better Auth.\n- The AI model is chosen by `LLM_PROVIDER`. The code's default is Gemini 2.5 Flash through OpenRouter, not Claude (step 9).\n\n**Why it matters:** these tours follow the code, not the README.\n\n**Watch out:** someone who reads your README and then your code will spot these. Know the real version, or update the README."
    },
    {
      "directory": "frontend",
      "title": "Part 1: the Next.js app",
      "description": "**What it is:** the web app. It does two jobs now:\n1. **Pages** you see in the browser, built with React, in `frontend/src/app` and `frontend/src/components`.\n2. **The API** (Hono), in `frontend/src/server`. It handles login checks, connected ad accounts, chat history, billing and more.\n\n**How it works:** Next.js sends pages to your browser. When a page needs data, it calls `/api/...` on the same site.\n\n**Why it matters:** one app, one deployment on Vercel. Pages and API share the same address.\n\n**Watch out:** the name `frontend` is misleading now. It holds a backend too."
    },
    {
      "file": "frontend/src/app/api/[[...route]]/route.ts",
      "line": 10,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 1,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 10,
          "character": 34
        }
      },
      "title": "Where the API plugs into Next.js",
      "description": "**What it is:** the 10 lines that put the whole Hono API inside Next.js.\n\n**How it works:** the folder name `[[...route]]` means *catch every address under /api*. Each exported function handles one HTTP method (GET, POST, …). `handle(app)` passes the request to the Hono app from `frontend/src/server/index.ts`.\n\n**Why it matters:** this is the Feb 9 change. It's why one Vercel deployment runs both pages and API. `maxDuration = 30` gives each API call up to 30 seconds on Vercel.\n\n**Watch out:** login addresses under `/api/auth/...` don't come here. Next.js picks the more specific route, `api/auth/[...path]`, which belongs to Neon Auth."
    },
    {
      "directory": "frontend/src/server",
      "title": "The API's code",
      "description": "**What it is:** the Hono API.\n- `index.ts` creates the app, adds shared middleware (logging, security headers, CORS) and mounts every route under `/api`.\n- `routes/` has one file per feature: `chat.ts`, `providers.ts`, `billing.ts`, `internal.ts`, …\n- `middleware/auth.ts` checks your login token on protected routes.\n- `db/` describes the database tables (Drizzle) in Neon Postgres.\n- `lib/` holds helpers: token checks, encryption, email, analytics.\n\n**Why it matters:** the agent calls this API too, to check your login and fetch your accounts and Google tokens. Those calls use `/internal/` routes, protected by a shared secret key (`X-Internal-Key`) instead of a login.\n\n**Watch out:** the same code also sits in `backend/api` (step 11). This folder is the live one."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat_server.py",
      "line": 178,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 168,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 178,
          "character": 10
        }
      },
      "title": "Part 2: the agent server",
      "description": "**What it is:** the Python service that answers chat messages. It's a separate server, built with FastAPI and hosted on Render.\n\n**How it works:** your browser sends the chat message **straight here**, not through Next.js. This endpoint first checks your login by asking the Next.js API, then starts the LangGraph run and **streams** the answer back piece by piece.\n\n**Why it matters:** an answer can take many seconds and several tool calls. Streaming lets you watch progress instead of waiting.\n\n**Watch out:** this is the one call that skips `/api`. It needs its own address (`NEXT_PUBLIC_AGENT_URL`) and its own CORS list (`ALLOWED_ORIGINS` in this file)."
    },
    {
      "directory": "backend/chat/graph",
      "title": "The agent's brain: LangGraph",
      "description": "**What it is:** the decision-making part of the agent, built with LangGraph.\n\n**How it works:** a message moves through four steps, called *nodes*:\n1. **router** works out the ad network and the kind of task (for example, AdMob reporting).\n2. **entity loader** loads your real accounts, apps and ad units.\n3. **specialist** is the AI model. It writes the answer or decides to call tools.\n4. **tool executor** runs the tools, then hands the results back to the specialist.\n\n**Why it matters:** each step stays simple, and the graph can loop (specialist ⇄ tools) until the answer is ready.\n\n**Watch out:** `nodes/` also has `verifier.py`, `synthesizer.py` and `tool_retriever.py`. The graph doesn't use those as nodes. Only the four above are wired up in `builder.py`. (The specialist does borrow one search helper from `tool_retriever.py`.)"
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/tools/loader.py",
      "line": 298,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 284,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 298,
          "character": 10
        }
      },
      "title": "Part 3: the tools",
      "description": "**What it is:** how the agent starts its tools.\n\n**How it works:** each ad network has its own small **MCP server**, in `backend/mcp_servers/providers/`. The agent starts the one it needs as a helper program (`python -m mcp_servers.providers.<network>`) and talks to it through standard input and output (`stdio`). Your Google access token goes in as `AUTH_TOKEN`, so the tools act on *your* account.\n\n**Why it matters:** the tools aren't written by hand. Each server is built from the network's own API description: Google's Discovery files for AdMob and Ad Manager, and OpenAPI files for the other seven.\n\n**Watch out:** `backend/mcp_servers/server.py`, the \"master\" server with 9 networks and 3 curated tools, is **not** what the agent uses. Only `run_mcp.py` starts it, for testing. Its curated tools are placeholders."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/graph/nodes/llm.py",
      "line": 30,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 19,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 30,
          "character": 51
        }
      },
      "title": "Which AI model?",
      "description": "**What it is:** the switch that picks the AI model.\n\n**How it works:** `LLM_PROVIDER` decides. The default is `openrouter`, and both roles map to **Gemini 2.5 Flash**: `haiku`, meant for fast jobs like routing, and `sonnet`, meant for harder ones. Setting `LLM_PROVIDER=anthropic` uses Claude directly.\n\n**Why it matters:** the names `haiku` and `sonnet` are only labels for *fast* and *strong*. They don't mean Claude is running.\n\n**Watch out:** your README says *Anthropic Claude*. What production really used depends on the environment variables on Render. Check before you say it in an interview."
    },
    {
