      "title": "Where it runs",
      "description": "**What it is:** the agent's health check, a tiny endpoint that only says *ok*.\n\n**How it works:** Render, which hosts the agent, calls `/` or `/health` regularly to check the service is alive. That's why it answers on both paths, for GET and HEAD requests.\n\n**Why it matters:** the full production picture is: Next.js app (pages + API) on **Vercel**, Python agent on **Render**, database on **Neon Postgres**. Tour 13 goes deeper.\n\n**Watch out:** your README mentions a `render.yaml`, but it isn't in the repo. The Render settings aren't stored in this code."
    },
    {
      "title": "What you can do now",
      "description": "### What you can do now\n\n- Name the three parts: the **Next.js app** (pages + API), the **agent** (Python, LangGraph), and the **tool servers** (MCP, one per network).\n- Say in one sentence how a question travels: browser → agent → router → entity loader → specialist ⇄ tools → answer streams back.\n- Point out what's out of date in the README.\n\n**Next:** *02 - Next.js basics on your app*. It explains how the pages are organised, using your own files."
    }
